[Music]  hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty  science chemistry phd cosmetic chemist  and  i have a lot of dead skin it's gotten a  lot worse during lockdown because  normally i keep my skin quite exfoliated  for pull but dance studios are closed so  i've just been letting myself turn into  a scaly snake just sort of growing a  cocoon around myself so when i found out  about these special exfoliating mitts  that make rolls of skin come off i got  pretty excited because i knew i was in  for a treat a disgusting treat i was  actually contacted by a journalist at  insider who wanted to do a story about  these exfoliating myths well we normally  have 10 to 20 layers of dead skin in our  stratum corneum this is the topmost  level of our epidermis these cells  normally shed in a process called  declamation but sometimes your skin  needs a bit of a nudge how well your  skin describes depends on a lot of  factors like age hydration the climate  you're in your skin care routine in  general moisturized skin sheds more  easily if you have too much dead skin  then it looks dry and flaky and dull  and it can also trap ingrown hairs these  mitts are sold as turkish bath mirrors  but i think they're actually used by a  lot of cultures around the middle east  and north africa they're traditionally  used after a steam bath when the skin is  really soft they're mostly advertised  for use on the body but some of them  also say that you can use them on your  face the most famous one is probably the  baiden glove biden glove it is 72  it has rave reviews it calls itself the  superior exfoliating glove and the  keywords on amazon include  dead skin highly recommend make sure  stretch marks dry skin worth every every  penny worth the money much dead so  overall very promising but right next to  it on amazon i also saw gloves that were  only eight dollars each so of course i  had to buy them all and try them out  there are actually two types of the  cheaper gloves there's one that is a lot  rougher and one that is a lot softer and  the listings didn't really give an  indication about how rough or soft they  were going to be the myths are made from  rayon fiber they have a weave that makes  them a balance of rough and soft so  rough enough to scrub off skin but soft  enough that you don't destroy your skin  they work a bit like really fine grit  sandpaper they mechanically buff away  dead skin cells so these are our three  contenders for  great mitt i guess  this is the baden mitten this is the  expensive one it comes in  i guess relatively fancy cardboard  packaging it's like a sachet that's  covered in  very direct marketing like  shopping tv style packaging  it has like a little  money back guarantee  seal in the corner it has papyrus font  is that how you say it i don't know  probably should have checked  it also comes with like the story of the  mitten inside it and like  it's got you know actual marketing copy  with instructions  in theory this is meant to last for two  years  now with the other two mittens obviously  they don't come in anywhere near as  interesting packaging they came in like  a sandwich bag  but i don't know i don't think i'd pay  all that extra money just for the  cardboard packaging although it is  probably a nice read so that's your  choice  with the texture  the expensive bait and mission it has a  fine sort of grit but it still feels  rough  with this one it has the same fine  fineness of grit but it feels a lot  smoother almost like it's been worn down  already  and with the third mitt it's a lot  rougher and a lot coarser as well so  we'll see how these go  in terms of results after using them a  bunch of times you can actually really  feel the difference between the fabric  the bait and glove feels a lot finer and  thinner these other two gloves are a bit  stiffer they don't feel like they're  quite as nicely woven i guess the bait  and glove definitely feels like it's  higher quality so the instructions say  to try these right after a shower and to  rub back and forth and not in circles i  tried out the baiden glove versus the  red glove on my shins the bait and glove  definitely gets the rolls off much  faster you can see there's a lot more  rolls but the red glove does pretty well  as well the black glove is a bit  underwhelming compared to the other two  you can see there are some rolls but it  takes a lot of effort so with all the  messing around with the cameras i think  my second shin dried out a bit before i  got to it so it might be a bit biased so  i tried again on my thighs but i did it  with the red and the black gloves first  so i tried the opposite order with the  cheaper gloves first with the black  glove i got a tiny bit with the red mint  i got some rolls and with the butter  mitt i got some rolls as well so i think  the red mitt is capable of getting these  rolls maybe not as much as the bait and  mitt my skin was super super soft  afterwards now all of these mitts say  you can use them on your face  but i'm really hesitant to use the red  one on my face because it feels so much  rougher than the other two  the beta mitt apparently is fine for  your face and i did end up using it on  one of my cheeks i did get some small  rolls off but i didn't press very hard  because i was pretty scared  and my cheek felt redder and hotter  afterwards but  it didn't really sting any more than my  other cheek when i put on products  afterwards so i don't know if i would  recommend using it on your face maybe if  you don't use any other exfoliating  products if you don't use any irritating  ingredients then using this maybe once a  week on your face with very gentle  pressure is fine but i think for most of  us people who use a lot of irritating  skin care products it's probably not the  best thing your barrier is probably  compromised enough already i also looked  at the weave of these under a microscope  so you can see that the beta mid does  have a different weave from the other  two and that might explain why it works  a bit better i also looked through the  roll cover glove through my microscope  you can see that the rolls are actually  dead skin yes this is what it looks like  if you put your eyeball right up to it  the mitt is intact the rolls don't look  like the fibers in the mid so it's very  different from a peeling gel where the  rolls are bunched up product now about  the claims the beta myth does claim to  be like microdermabrasion and i think  that might be a bit much i really don't  think this is going to do as much as  microdermabrasion  maybe if they had a study where they  compared the two head to head i'd be  okay with that comparison but as it  stands i think it is  pretty exaggerated so it says there's an  immediate improvement in blackheads and  large pores i think with black as maybe  you might dislodge them especially after  the steam from the shower but i don't  think you'll do that much blackheads are  oxidized sebum and cells inside your  pores they're not really on the surface  this sort of exfoliation is very surface  level i think you would get much more  benefit if you use something like a  chemical exfoliant that can get deeper  into the pores same with large pores i  don't think it's going to do very much  with firmness and wrinkles i think this  does increase the circulation which can  make your skin temporarily more firm  it's not going to do anything like  stimulate collagen for example though  now with wrinkles i think exfoliation in  general does help the appearance of  wrinkles and that's simply because if  you have a whole bunch of dead skin it  can make wrinkles look a lot coarser  scars stretch marks skin tone and  dryness i mean it might make them look a  bit better because you're helping the  surface look better but it's not going  to have a really big effect now scars  and stretch marks  massage in general can reduce stretch  masks and it can soften up scars  but  beyond that i don't think the glove is  doing anything special exfoliation can  also make them look better but again  it's not some sort of magical property  of the glove skin tone exfoliating can  get rid of maybe some cells on the  surface but i don't think it's going to  make a massive difference not like the  difference that a proper active like  lactic acid could it can definitely help  remove dry flaky skin detox that's  always a red flag for pseudoscience  discolorations that's really vague even  tanning will definitely work fake tan  reacts with dead skin so if you have a  lot of dead skin in one area and not  somewhere else you end up with a patchy  tan and so exfoliating before a tan is  always a good idea acne control i guess  all exfoliation can help a bit with acne  but  that is a pretty big claim for something  that's just a physical exfoliant so are  these gloves too rough on your skin  well i think it really depends one of  the issues with these is that it is  really prone to user error it's very  exciting to be rubbing off all of this  dead skin but it turns out that you need  dead skin those 10 to 20 layers on the  surface of your skin are there to  protect the living layers of your skin  so if you get rid of too many then you  end up with a really compromised barrier  you end up with stinging and irritation  and it takes a while to build that  barrier back up so you need to practice  a bit of self-control with these you  can't go too hard you can't use them too  often i think it's especially dangerous  on your face because a lot of us already  use active ingredients that can pack  down our stratum corneum they're already  exfoliating for example and so if you're  using this on top of all those  exfoliants then you're probably getting  rid of too much skin that means that you  end up with a compromised barrier and  that means irritation sensitivity your  face skin is also thinner and so you've  got less to take off to start with so  after i tried this mid out on my face on  this half only i noticed there was a  patch of skin here that was a bit dry  and flaky and it also stung a bit when i  put sunscreen on it for about a week  afterwards i thought i used it pretty  gently just hard enough to get some  rolls so yeah over exfoliating is really  easy with these mitts i wouldn't  recommend using it on your face if you  use any other exfoliating actors maybe  any other irritating actors which i  think is probably everyone who is deep  enough down the skincare rabbit hole to  be watching my channel so for face  exfoliation i would probably recommend  something different such as a chemical  exfoliant or maybe a scrub  probably not these definitely not the  rougher ones you also have to be careful  when looking after these you want to let  these dry out properly in between uses  the reason is after you've rubbed off  all of this dead skin  dead skin is really good food for  bacteria and when you have moisture and  dead skin they grow and so you end up  with lots of bacteria on these then you  rub them back onto your skin into your  skin and you end up with a bacterial  infection the most common one is  pseudomonas folliculitis this is when  the bacteria end up in your hair  follicles and it looks a little bit like  tiny red pimples with pus in them that  is bacteria not the kind of gross we  want so is the 70 brand name version  worth it i think it does have a much  nicer texture than the cheaper ones it  feels a lot more effective without being  too rough it also feels a lot better  constructed so it should last a bit  longer it says it'll last up to two  years according to the packet but i  think the cheaper ones can definitely  still do the job but it might take a few  goes to find the right one you might end  up with one that doesn't really do much  you might end up with one that is a bit  too rough and you'll have to use it a  lot more gently i think if i wasn't  doing this for a video i would probably  just buy five cheaper ones and see which  one worked best so i hope you like this  slightly disgusting video you can follow  me on instagram at lab muffin beauty  science and check out my blog for more  about the science behind beauty products  see you next time to nerd out more and i  will try not to talk about disgusting  dead skin

